# The Gaudin $B$ operator and the jet basis {#sec:B}

**Punchline.** There is no single Gaudin $B$ operator with simple spectrum. The XXX operator $B(u)$ becomes, as $h\to0$, a hierarchy $\nu^3[1+hb_1+h^2b_2+\dots]$ whose first member is Cartan-like and degenerate; the SoV basis becomes a $\zeta$-independent \emph{jet basis}, defined by a wavefunction rule in terms of eight one-site monomials, and the kernel $G$ of the jet formula is exactly the K tensor of the vacuum family in that basis. Where the degeneracy lives is the diagonal frame, not $B$.

## The XXX operator and its eigenbasis {#sec:Bxxx}

In the new XXX conventions (gl(3), untwisted) $B(u)=T_{11}(u)\,\mathrm{qmin}_{12,12}(u+h)+T_{21}(u)\,\mathrm{qmin}_{12,13}(u+h)$. It has simple spectrum $\prod_s\prod_k(u-\theta_s-m_kh)$ with labels $(a|bc)$ per site and roots $\theta_s+\{-1-a,-b,-c\}h$, $\lambda_2\le a\le\lambda_1$, $0\le c\le\lambda_2$, $c\le b\le a$; for the adjoint there are eight labels per site, $(2,2,1)$ being the vacuum with roots $\{-3,-2,-1\}$. Its left eigenbasis is the SoV basis, in which the wavefunction is $\langle x|\Psi_c\rangle/\langle x_0|\Psi_c\rangle=N_x\prod_sM^{(s)}_x/M^{(s)}_{\rm vac}$ with $M_{(a|bc)}=\mathrm{minor}_s(-1-a,-b)\,Q^3(\theta_s-ch)$. We verified, exactly at $h=1/2$ on the adjoint, that $B$ has degree $6$ in $u$ and in $h$, $[B(u_0),B(u_1)]=0$, all 64 label-rule eigenvalues are simple and the left eigencovectors are $u$-independent.^[Agent D3, `RESULTS-gaudin-B.md` B0 (Fractions, $u_0=3/7$, $u_1=-2/9$; nnz of $B_0,\dots,B_6$ are $64,64,232,251,247,178,75$). The XXX wavefunction check with dual Q's at $h=1/2$ was skipped as allowed; the rule is not used by the derivation, and the formula built on it is reproduced independently.]

As $h\to0$ all roots collapse to $\theta_s$. So the "Gaudin $B$" cannot be one operator with simple spectrum; it has to be a hierarchy whose joint generalised eigenbasis is a jet basis, the $h$-expansion coefficients (divided differences) of the SoV covectors.

## The hierarchy $b_1,b_2,\dots$ {#sec:bk}

With $t(x)=T(x)/\nu(x)=1+hL(x)+h^2C(x)$, $\nu=\Pi$, $L_{ij}=\sum_sE^{s}_{ij}/(x-\theta_s)$ the Gaudin Lax matrix and $C_{ij}=\sum_kE^1_{ik}E^2_{kj}/((x-\theta_1)(x-\theta_2))$ the cross-site term, the exact all-order structure is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac B{\nu^3}=(1+hs_1+h^2s_2)\Bigl\{&t_{11}(u)\bigl[t_{11}(u+h)t_{22}(u)-t_{21}(u+h)t_{12}(u)\bigr]\\
&+t_{21}(u)\bigl[t_{11}(u+h)t_{23}(u)-t_{21}(u+h)t_{13}(u)\bigr]\Bigr\},
\end{aligned}\label{eq:Bexact}
$$
$s_1=\nu'/\nu$, $s_2=1/((u-\theta_1)(u-\theta_2))$, checked against the chain for $b_0,\dots,b_6$ at three values of $u$ (orders 7, 8 vanish). Expanding $B/\nu^3=1+hb_1+h^2b_2+\dots$,
$$
b_1=2L_{11}+L_{22}+\frac{\nu'}\nu,\qquad b_2=2C_{11}+C_{22}+L_{11}'+L_{11}^2+2L_{11}L_{22}-L_{21}L_{12}+L_{21}L_{23}+\frac{\nu'}\nu(2L_{11}+L_{22})+s_2.\label{eq:b12}
$$
The first, $b_1$, is diagonal (Cartan) with eigenvalue $\sum_s(1+a+b+c)/(u-\theta_s)$ and has 25 eigenspaces of dimensions $1$ (4 times), $2$ (12 times) and $4$ (9 times): it does not resolve the 64 labels. $b_2$ needs the cross-site $C_{ij}$: it lies outside the span of $\{L_{ij}L_{kl},L',L'',L,1\}$ (171 residual entries) and inside it once the $C_{ij}$ are added. Further facts, all exact:

- the $b_k(u)$ do not commute individually, $[b_i(u),b_j(v)]\neq0$ for all $(i,j)$ except $(1,1)$ and $(6,6)$, but $\sum_{i+j=n}[b_i(u),b_j(v)]=0$ for $n=2,\dots,8$;
- compressed to the $b_1$-eigenspaces, $b_2$ has as eigenvalues the $h^2$-coefficient of the label-rule series (25/25 eigenspaces) and splits every eigenspace completely: the 64 pairs $(b_1,b_2^{\rm compressed})$ are distinct; $b_2$ alone has 62 simple roots and one double root with a Jordan block (the $b_k$ are not individually diagonalisable);
- $B$ commutes with the grading $H_{\rm tot}=\sum_s(2E^s_{11}+E^s_{22})$, with level block sizes $1,4,8,12,14,12,8,4,1$, and every SoV covector lives in the level $D(x)=\sum_s(6-(1+a+b+c))$;
- the Lie algebra generated by all coefficient matrices $B^{(m)}_k$ is \emph{solvable} on every level block (derived series: $80\to67\to45\to10\to0$ on the 14-block), so the $b_k(u)$ are simultaneously triangularisable block by block, with the level flag.
- **No single polynomial $B_G(u)$ of degree $\le3$ in $L,L'$ triangularises the jet basis** (tried: $b_1,b_2,b_3,b_2-b_1^2/2$ in the jet basis; $b_1$ has only 25 distinct values and is not triangular inside level blocks). The operator that does resolve all 64 labels is the $b_1$-block compression of $b_2$, which needs the projectors onto $H_1,H_2$ eigenspaces: not a polynomial of small degree in $L$.^[Agent D3, `RESULTS-gaudin-B.md` B1, B5.]

## Where the degeneracy lives {#sec:degeneracy}

We expected degeneracy in $B$: at $h\to0$ all $64$ SoV covectors become proportional. That is refuted. In the XXX companion frame the 64 covectors $\langle x|(h)$, normalised by $\langle x|r=1$, are rational in $h$ of degree $\le5$ over $\le5$ (common denominator; degree tally $0:11,\,1:23,\,2:19,\,3:7,\,4:3,\,5:1$), validated at six unseen rational $h$ (64/64), and their \emph{leading terms at $h=0$ are pairwise distinct and linearly independent (rank 64)}. The degeneracy lives in the diagonal frame, where the Gaudin states live: there the covectors are $f^d_x(h)=f_x(h)(P\otimes P)$ with $P=P_{\rm phys}^{-1}(e^{h\zeta})$, every entry $O(h)$ with singular values $h(1,h,h,h^2,h^2,h^3,h^3,h^4)$. All 64 have valuation $h^2$ and \emph{all 64 leading covectors are proportional to $x^G\otimes x^G$} (rank 1). The ranks of the span of Taylor coefficients of $f^d_x/h^2$ to order $K=0,\dots,8$ are $1,5,13,25,39,51,59,63,64$. Saturating over $\mathbb{Q}[[h]]$ by echelon reduction (dividing by $h$ when the leading term vanishes) yields the \emph{jet basis} with defects per level $1,4,8,12,14,12,8,4,1$, the coefficients of $(1+2t+2t^2+2t^3+t^4)^2$: per site eight jets of weights $0,1,1,2,2,3,3,4$, the one-site depth of $P_{\rm phys}$.^[Agent D3, `RESULTS-gaudin-B.md` B2, at $\zeta=P,P',X_1$.]

## The canonical jet basis and its wavefunction {#sec:canjet}

The jet basis is made canonical by the wavefunction rule. Its one-site monomials are, in terms of the dual Q-functions at $\theta_s$,
$$
\begin{aligned}
m_0&=P^3p_{02},& m_1&=P^{3\prime}p_{02},& m_2&=P^3p_{03},& m_3&=P^{3\prime}p_{03},\\
m_4&=P^{3\prime}p_{12},& m_5&=P^3p_{23},& m_6&=P^{3\prime}p_{13},& m_7&=P^{3\prime}p_{23},
\end{aligned}\label{eq:m8}
$$
with weights $w=c+k+l=2,3,3,4,4,5,5,6$: eight monomials, one per site label ($=\dim V$). These are what the twelve $P^{3(c)}p_{kl}$, $c\le1$, $l\le3$ reduce to ($p_{01}=0$, $p_{12}=\kappa p_{02}$, $p_{13}=\kappa p_{03}$). The 64 products $m_i(\theta_1)m_j(\theta_2)$ are independent functions on the 64 states ($\sigma_{\min}/\sigma_{\max}=6\cdot10^{-12}$). The canonical jet covectors $\langle i,j|^c$ are defined by
$$
\frac{\langle i,j|^c\Psi_c\rangle}{\langle x_0|\Psi_c\rangle}=\frac{m_i(\theta_1)\,m_j(\theta_2)}{M_1M_2},\label{eq:wfrule}
$$
in the Gaudin companion frame ($\Psi_c=(P_{\rm phys}^{-1}\otimes P_{\rm phys}^{-1})|_{z=\zeta}\Psi_d$). The $64\times64$ matrix $Y$ expressing them in the physical basis is \emph{exactly $\zeta$-independent and rational}: identical exact rationals from the states at $P$, $P'$, $X_1$ (833 non-zero entries, inverse 975). Only five of the 64 covectors are product covectors (the $8\times8$ coefficient matrix has rank 1): $(0,0),(0,1),(0,2),(1,0),(2,0)$; the other 59 couple the two sites through $1/\theta_{12}$ (ranks $2$ to $8$). Examples: $\langle00|=e_{00}$, $\langle01|=-e_{01}$, $\langle02|=-2e_{02}+e_{01}-2t\,e_{00}$ with $t=1/\theta_{12}$. The rule (\ref{eq:wfrule}) was verified to $6\cdot10^{-76}$ ($P$), $1.4\cdot10^{-74}$ ($P'$), $1.5\cdot10^{-73}$ ($X_1$) on all $64\times64$ with $Y$ built at $P$ only; the saturation basis is block-triangular with respect to it, the filtration being the jet-weight filtration (level $d=w_i+w_j-4$).^[Agent D3, `RESULTS-gaudin-B.md` B3. The unipotent gauge freedom inside each filtration level is irrelevant for $G$ (a bilinear form on jets, not on the basis). A determined solve at $P$ is the weakest evidence; the independent evidence is rationality, exact triangularity, $\zeta$-independence and the match with $G$.]

## The K tensor and the derivation of $G$ {#sec:Ktensor}

Expanding the companion-frame family $\Phi_c^\infty=\Omega_c^\infty[a]\otimes\Omega_c^\infty[a']$ in the canonical jet basis gives its K tensor $\Theta_\mu=(\Omega_c^\infty[a]\otimes\Omega_c^\infty[a'])Y^{-1}$, with exactly \emph{25 non-zero entries out of 64}, all on $N_5\times N_5$ (in the saturation basis: 36 non-zero entries). Writing $\Theta_{ij}=\Theta(a,a',1/\theta_{12})$ one has
$$
\Theta_{ij}(a,a',t)=\frac{G_{ij}(1/a,1/a',t)}{4\,\omega_1^3\omega_1'^3}\quad\text{(adjoint), i.e. }\ \Theta=\frac{K}{(\omega_1\omega_1')^{2\lambda_1-\lambda_2}},\label{eq:Theta}
$$
as an exact polynomial identity in $(a,a')$, 25/25 entries, at three values of $\theta_{12}$ ($31/35,29/45,5/6$). This is the third derivation of the kernel. The kernel has rank $1$ at $1/\theta_{12}=0$ (the sites decouple, $G$ becomes a product) and rank $5$ otherwise, so the whole site coupling is through $1/\theta_{12}$. For equal twist (finite $\omega_2$) all 64 jet coefficients are non-zero (levels $1,4,8,12,14,12,8,4,1$) and the jet expansion reproduces $\langle\Phi|\psi\rangle/\langle x_0|\psi\rangle$ to $6\cdot10^{-75}$; moreover $Q_{\rm num}^{\rm eq}M_1M_2/\det N_B(\omega)$ is state-independent (spread $1.2\cdot10^{-75}$, value $12.5381319888976$ at $\zeta=P$, $\theta=(2/7,-3/5)$), i.e. the $6\times6$ determinant of Section~\ref{sec:detN} is a Q-number of the same kind.^[Agent D3, `RESULTS-gaudin-B.md` B4. The reproduction of the plan's expectation that the kernel is the "K tensor times wavefunction monomials" was the main goal of round 3; the origin of $c$ is the frame tie $\Omega_c^\infty[1/\omega]P_{\rm phys}^{-1}(z=\zeta)=\Omega_D^\infty[\zeta,\omega]/\kappa^G$ exactly.]

# The overlap as a function of the Laurent data {#sec:nu}

**Punchline.** Every Plücker monomial is $M$ times a rational function of the Laurent data $(H_s,a_s,b_s)$ of the state at the two sites, so $c\,Q_{\rm num}=\sum_{ij}G^s_{ij}\nu_i(\theta_1)\nu_j(\theta_2)$ and neither the Q polynomials nor their normalisation enter: the Q-number is a function of eigenvalue data.

## The Frobenius reduction (adjoint) {#sec:frobred}

Near $\theta_s$, with $x=u-\theta_s$ and exponents $\{0,2,4\}$ for the adjoint, every solution is $P^j=A_jf_0+B_jf_2+C_jf_4$ with Frobenius series
$$
f_0=1+\kappa x+\gamma_2x^2+\gamma_3x^3+\dots,\qquad f_2=x^2+d_3x^3+\dots.\label{eq:fseries}
$$
To derivative order $3$ only $f_0,f_2$ matter, and the Plücker coordinates are $p_{kl}=[A,B]_{23}\,w_{kl}$ with $[A,B]_{23}=A_2B_3-A_3B_2$ and $w_{02}=2$, $w_{03}=6d_3$, $w_{12}=2\kappa$, $w_{23}=12(\gamma_2d_3-\gamma_3)$, $w_{01}=0$. Hence $N_i=M\nu_i$ with
$$
\nu=(1,\ \kappa,\ \pi,\ \kappa\pi,\ \varepsilon_s),\qquad \pi=\frac{p_{03}}{p_{02}}=3d_3,\qquad \varepsilon_s=\frac{p_{23}}{p_{02}}=6(\gamma_2d_3-\gamma_3),\label{eq:nu}
$$
and $M$ cancels from the left-hand side of (\ref{eq:jet}):
$$
c\,Q_{\rm num}=\sum_{ij}G^s_{ij}(\omega_1,\omega_1',\theta_{12})\,\nu_i(\theta_1)\,\nu_j(\theta_2),\qquad G^s=G/4.\label{eq:nuform}
$$
The Frobenius recursion, with $\delta=\theta_s-\theta_t$ ($t\ne s$), $\sigma_1=\sum\zeta$, $e_2=e_2(\zeta)$, $e_3=e_3(\zeta)$, gives exactly (sympy)
$$
\begin{aligned}
\kappa&=\frac{a_s}3,\qquad \pi=2H_s-a_s-2\sigma_1-\frac6\delta,\\
\varepsilon_s&=-\frac23\Bigl[\frac{H_ta_s}\delta-a_sb_s+a_se_2+\frac{3a_s}{\delta^2}-\frac{3a_t}{\delta^2}-\frac{3b_t}\delta-3e_3\Bigr],\qquad b_s=\frac{a_s(H_s-a_s)}3,
\end{aligned}\label{eq:nulaurent}
$$
the last being the first no-log condition (\ref{eq:nolog1}) with $c_2=3$. We verified (\ref{eq:nulaurent}) against $N_i/M$ built from the Q polynomials to $10^{-174}$--$10^{-193}$ on all six regime branches, both sites, at $\epsilon=10^{-6}/8$, and the no-log relation to $10^{-199}$. Thus $Q_{\rm num}$ is a function of $(H_1,a_1,H_2,a_2)$, $\zeta$, $\theta_{12}$, $\omega_1,\omega_1'$ only.^[Agent U2, `RESULTS-untwist-limit.md` L3 and `untwist_limit_frob.py`; independently re-verified by U1 (`RESULTS-untwisted-Q.md` T2: the $\nu$-formulas hold to $10^{-185}$ for all six branches, both sites). The result needs no use of the Q polynomials; it was reached by "the Frobenius mechanism", which is representation-generic.] The reduction also improves the limit identity by 14 or more digits (the residuals of Section~\ref{sec:untwist} drop from $10^{-43}$--$10^{-75}$ to $10^{-55}$--$10^{-157}$) and requires $\epsilon$-orders $\le5$ instead of $8$.

## Why this must hold for every $\Lambda$ {#sec:nugen}

The argument is local. For exponents $\{0,\lambda_2+1,\lambda_1+2\}$ the jet of every solution up to order $\lambda_1+1$ lies in the 2-plane spanned by the exponent-0 and the exponent-$(\lambda_2+1)$ Frobenius vectors; the coefficients of these vectors are rational functions of $(H_s,a_s,b_s)$, the other site's data through the regular parts, and $e_k(\zeta)$. Hence every non-forced $p_{kl}=D_s\,\nu_{kl}$ with $D_s$ the $2\times2$ determinant of the leading components of the two non-trivial solutions (the analogue of $[A,B]_{23}$) and $\nu_{kl}$ rational, and $M=P^3D_s\nu_{0,\lambda_2+1}$, so that $P^3D_s$ cancels between the sides. We have \emph{not} derived the $\nu_{kl}$ for $\lambda_1\ge3$, and the untwisting limit for $[3,1,0]$, $[3,2,0]$ is not done; the general statement is an expectation based on the adjoint and on the verified exponent and jet-rank structure (Section~\ref{sec:frob}). This is work package B of round 5.
